  ('zcrm_1579374000128009138', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000128033004', 'Lora Paulson', NULL, '11-3-23 Lora found me via the Naturally Inspired Radio - Jason and Tammy Garcia. However, her oldest daughter 17 has hemocromotosis which is a disorder where her body/blood holds onto too much iron. The solution is to donate blood every 3 months. She seems to think she can donate the blood even if it''s not usable. Sounds concerning. She will have to regularly manage this. If it''s well managed it''s less likely to develop into diabetes, heart issues, etc. Lora is currently going to a functional medicine doc bc she has some of the symptoms of this disease. She''s learned it''s genetic and it may come from her. Her youngest daughter has a VP shunt to drain fluid from her brain! This has been going on for 3 years. Finally her husband is healthy and nothing to report. I suggested that they stay on their insurance through Lora''s work. It only costs her $187 per pay period. So, her employer is paying most of the cost already. She just wants better choices of drs. and alternative care. But, MCS is way too risky for them. Her husband gets his insurance through his employer and he sounds less interested in MCS. Assuming his employer is paying for part of his insurance, he may stay put as well. She will let me know.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2023-11-03 14:38:23', '2023-11-03 14:38:23'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000127963008', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000127968001', 'Mark Bartorelli', NULL, '11-2-23 Mark is 65 but his wife is 59. He''s looking for her. He found me via the Naturally Inspired Podcast, Tammy and Jason Garcia. He used to be an healthcare actuary and once he saw what was happening during covid, he never wants to use the traditional medical system again, unless it''s for legit ER, acute scenario. Claims went way down during covid and now they are skyrocketing with all of the deaths and illnesses from the experimental treatments. He went to St Mike''s College in BTV! Lives in PA and understands most healthshares aren''t allowed in PA but CARE+ is allowed so far. He watches the Highwire all the time. He also watched our 9 minute video. He will probably wait until April of 2024 to get his wife enrolled. He had great questions.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2023-11-02 15:37:16', '2023-11-02 15:37:16'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000127844008', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000127861001', 'Jess Feaster', NULL, '11-1-23 Jess is 42, Jay 49 and turning 50 in Aug. 2024 and 11 year old twins. They are self-employed and have 1 employee but not sure if they will offer anything. They are paying $1500 now. They do their annual wellness visits and used to have an HSA account. Jess has sarcoidosis which is an inflammatory disease. She''s not sure what could go wrong with it and will decide if it''s a concern. She takes hydroxychloroquine every way which is super cheap. She also takes Restasis which is  - Restasis Eye Drops 0.05% (Cyclosporine) - 60 x 0.4ml single-use vials, brand- $164.80+$9.95 (shipping)
Restasis Eye Drops 0.05% (Cyclosporine) - 90 x 0.4ml single-use vials, brand- $223.20+$9.95. It looks possibly cheaper through PharmacyChecker.com. This brought it down to about $77 a month which is amenable. She goes to a pulmonogist 2x a year and gets an EKG every year plus blood tests. She already pays most of the full cost for these with her current ins and for double the cost of ins. The Dr. visit was about $250 she thinks.
Jay has sleep apnea. I read the sleep apnea section from the Sharing Guidelines.  They currently use Delta Dental and want vision. They want Dr. Rachel Hills and she''s not showing up in the network. Dr. Lariss Applegate is showing up for the kids.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2023-11-01 15:16:51', '2023-11-01 15:20:17'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000127729018', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000127739032', 'Bonnie McGrath', NULL, '10-24-23 and 10-31-23 Bonnie 62 came to me via Brent Kruehl. She lives in MN and thought she lost Cobra. She''s self-employed, no major pre-x. Takes lisinopril for high blood pressure, does wellness visits and has never had an HSA but would like one. She also takes 5 mg of rosathans(?) for high cholesterol. She really likes the life care for her daughter. She''s paying $1077 for her or $1918 for adding her daughter who lives in AZ. She was ready to enroll today until some folks moved mountains to get her onto Cobra. Out of respect for all they did, she will stay with Cobra for a few months and then plans to move to MPB in the new year.  She likes SECURE HSA and she knows the price will increase modestly but nothing in comparison to what she''s paying now. She also does skin checks but said nothing is on watch right now, so not concerned.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2023-10-31 16:41:20', '2023-10-31 16:41:20'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000127297003', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000127296001', 'Karen Thorpe', NULL, '10-25-23 Karen is 53 and self-employed. She has Anthem BC/BS now since she had an ankle surgery. She was on Christian Healthshare and/or Medishare previously. She felt it would b easier to deal with her surgery with traditional insurance. She''s still doing PT on it now and knows it''s pre-x and she will have to pay the full cost of anything related in the first year. She understands the tiered benefits onward. She''s super healthy, no other pre-x, no tobacco. She''s terrified of doctors and does not do the annual wellness screenings. I think she said she did Cologuard on her own but otherwise avoids all of the traditional screenings. I let her know if she joins for Dec. 1st, she''ll be grandfathered in to the current pricing at $265/$1000 plus the $125/$25. She''s still exploring the other healthshares and will let me know before Nov. 20th if she wants to join for Dec. 1st. She also knows it''s open enrollment and to reach out as soon as possible. She came to me via Kimberly Baldwin.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2023-10-25 12:37:58', '2023-10-25 12:37:58'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000127140045', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000118020047', 'Paolo Bonetti', NULL, '10-23-23 Spoke with Paolo, Mark Maggio Evan Walters, Drew Grumhaus about possibly group option with MCS and ins via ACA or via ICHRA. They have 17 eligible employees. 14 of the 17 took their plan. The plan is free to the employee and they may pay a small portion of the spouse or family members. I did presentation with Steve Alley for ICHRA option. However, can''t use MEC with ICHRA. So, if they want the tax benefits, we will have to present the Health Access for preventative instead. He preferred to have a census and just fact finding in the initial meeting. I dove right in to MCS. We''ll see. Evan wants it to be easy for his employees and for their staff. His concerns are needing to collect the bills and track and submit them. I think they see the value otherwise. They will talk amongst themselves and keep me posted.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2023-10-23 17:28:40', '2023-10-23 17:28:40'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000126712037', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000126694031', 'Karen Dame', NULL, '10.17.2023 Karen Dame called the concierge line, and said "I had already left a message with someone, and they told me that someone will call me back in an hour". Then she proceeded to share that she heard about MPB through a FB group, and wanted to know more about the company. I shared with her that this is indeed a health-sharing company and differs us from many other companies is the extended services that we offer with each plan, and how some plans have preventative insurance embedded. 
