  ('zcrm_1579374000087383003', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000087331283', 'Marissa Tulaski', NULL, '2-24-22 Marissa is 30 and referred by Sola De Dominico who I assume is the Sola from BSU. She''s not a member. Marissa is young and healthy but she did have a uterine fybroid surgery in July 2021. She doesn''t typically do her annual wellness visit but feels like she should soon. She also prefers to see naturopathic docs, chiropractic care, etc. She just cancelled her traditional ins 10 minutes ago since they don''t allow for alternative care or include dental or vision. She was paying $215 for that with an assumed high ded and mop. She likes the sounds of this and knows the earliest start date is April 1st. She''s a W2 employee for a non-profit org that does not offer ins. They might be interested in doing so with something like this. She''s only eligible for CARE+ at this point bc doesn''t have any other source of income of self-employment.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-24 14:36:45', '2022-02-24 14:36:45'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000087280038', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000087289031', 'Jade Chen', NULL, '2-23-22 Jade is 55 and husband is 60. He''s considering leaving his job within next  6 months due to stress and tired of corporate world. He has meds for high blood pressure. No hospital visits or anything, it''s well managed. Jade has bi-polar and asked many great questions about virtual healthcare and Life Care to understand difference and to it works and who to go to. She understands the 12 visits and breaks between. If she needs to continue she can pay cash to do so. She also understands virtual healthcare with Behavioral Health Specialists are the MD to help on a more damage control level, not for therapy sessions for this portion of the membership. She knows she may have to pay cash for in-person visits with her current MD for bi-polar. Her 4 meds are all generic and looking to be under $10 for each through GoodRX or mail order, etc. She also just had her colonoscopy 1 year ago and not again for another 9 years. Husband has never had one to her knowledge. Meds lorazapem, sertraline, lamotrigine and zyprexa. She knows of 20th of the month deadline and $625/$1000 SECURE membership. She has a side job she can start again to be compliant with SECURE.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-23 15:53:14', '2022-02-23 16:03:26'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000087196029', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000087189606', 'Jack Heimsoth', NULL, '2-22-22 Jack is 45 and DeAnna is 46. She works for BVSD and has own side biz for many years. Jack is self-employed. They currently have Kaiser for the past 10 1/2 years and pay $1000 month now. She doesn''t know her ded or mop. They barely ever use their health ins. She''s not super knowledgeable about health ins and all of the terminology. She likes the security of Kaiser but turns out her last experience with a doctor there was "wretched." So, she''s not eager to use them. They prefer acupuncture, chiro, massage, etc.  I forgot to mention ability to seek alternative care with pre-approval and self-pay aspect. But, she''s willing to watch the video now and read the informational email. They don''t typically do their annual wellness visits but they do have an HSA. So, CARE+ or SECURE are good options. They are as natural as possible, especially with Bella, 8 with no shots.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-22 17:44:32', '2022-02-22 17:44:32'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000087196024', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000081767001', 'Christina Turco', NULL, '2-22-22 Looking at Delta Dental Clear Plan for $54.75. No waiting period and set fees for each services needed. This will help her for current dental issues which she might need a filling right away and possibly a crown. Later may need a root canal. An old crown fell. She might need an extraction or implant or bridge in the future. Wants to see Broadway Smiles with Tara Rathburn and oral surgeons Dr. Thao Le or Dr. Fowley. Both are showing in the Delta Dental PPO Premier network. Looks positive but I told her to call to double check. She will do so and we will talk tomorrow at 3:00 for appt to enroll.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-22 17:35:46', '2022-02-22 17:35:46'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000087196008', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000087189053', 'Brandon Dills', NULL, '2-22-22 Katie is calling for Brandon Dills and they just want dental and short-term and long-term disability. I presented the HMA and touched on Living Benefits concept only. I have created to illustrations for LB, one at $100K and the other at $250K as a starting point.  No dental issues but uses tobacco! Estimated monthly income is $6K and expenses only $3K and no debts. No serious family history of anything except his father had some polyps that were not cancer removed. They are also getting health ins through marketplace for only $150 a month and are all set with vision.  I''m inviting Andre to join me to discuss LB and he''s licensed in MD.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-22 10:36:55', '2022-02-22 10:38:17'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000086307003', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000086305001', 'Rui Correai', NULL, '2-7-22 Rui is 53 and works for Natalie Joly at Mirabelle Restaurant. Natalie will pay 50% and he will pay 50%. He''s active and skis a lot. Goes to chiro as preventative 1-2x a month. Knee bothering him but no doctor visits. I reiterated the pre-x clause and the symptomatic portion of the clause means pre-x. I let him know that anything in his chiro records will be deemed pre-x. I''m not sure what he''s treating, if anything at chiro. Not running as much as he''d like. No meds, no tobacco, no health ins for 3 years. Daughter has health ins and son might need it. He''s turning 27 this month. He wants SECURE. $275/$1000 and knows of price increase to $325 in Sept for Oct membership.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-10 09:56:32', '2022-02-10 09:56:32'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000086042003', 'lead', 'zcrm_1579374000086041001', 'Laurie Love', NULL, '2-3-22 Hi Wendy,

Thanks so much for getting back to me, and I have some updates for you. 

Luckily, I was able to chat with a health coverage specialist in Larimer County today--closest I could get to a Medicaid or DHS rep, as it''s been impossible to get in contact with someone from Boulder County. I found out some surprising news-- that in January, the State extended the Public Health emergency insurance (Medicaid or Health First) through April. She said that means that everyone on Medicaid stays on Medicaid until the public health emergency is officially over (unless they opt-out which would leave those who do without health insurance or a gap in coverage). She said because of this to stay on Medicaid until I get an official letter from them that will state when the official date of the public health emergency will end. She said legally, they must give 60 days from the date of that letter to transition to new insurance, and that they will be providing a special 60 day enrollment period to transition to a new insurance plan. She said this process will likely take some time to transition everyone from Medicaid to new plans (perhaps even up to a year). And she also mentioned that because I''ve been on medicaid for 5 years, there''s a chance they will put me on ''transitional Medicaid''. 

She was also able to help me with my income updates in PEAK. I also emailed Boulder County DHS to verify everything and they should get back to me in a couple business days. For now, this rep said to not worry about doing anything further until I get official correspondence from my county that I can switch insurances (she said otherwise I could chance having a gap in coverage). 

 Also as a sidenote--I told her that I was working with a broker, and had mentioned that you had needed a disqualification letter from Medicaid before switching me to a new insurance, and she said that that is no longer needed to transfer to a new plan. She said they have been undergoing rapid changes with the rules, and just told me to pass along the info. 

Once I get more information I will follow up again with you. I really appreciate your help--this has been a very confusing situation and it''s a huge change for me so I just want to make sure I''m covered. 

Thank you so much again and have a wonderful day,
Laurie', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2022-02-07 11:01:13', '2022-02-07 11:01:13'),
