  ('zcrm_1579374000180318057', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000176281018', 'Kelly Benson', NULL, 'Welcome call complete. V.O.', 'Concierge', 'Concierge', '2026-02-09 07:22:14', '2026-02-09 07:22:14'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000180318037', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000180285002', 'Matthew Prestero', 'Member Only $5000 IUA Care+', NULL, 'Michele R', 'Michele R', '2026-02-09 06:28:32', '2026-02-09 06:28:32'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000180284006', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000120814004', 'Reid Collins', NULL, '2-6-26 Reid was hoping for  a refund of his $50 for Essentials all the way back to Feb or March of 2025 bc he didn''t feel supported and it took so long. He made it sound like Sarah only helped him one or twice and left the rest up to him. However, Sarah has incredibly well documented communications with him over the course of the entire year. He got the debt dismissed as well! Looks like the debt was about $100K!<br><br>Hi Wendy,<br>Bryan is no longer with the company, but I’m happy to walk through what happened and provide some context around the timeline so everything is clear.<br>Back on <b>2/6/25</b>, Bryan and I had a call with Reid to review the documents needed and to go over how the Debt Dismissal process works. At that time, we explained that once the application was submitted, the hospital would communicate directly with Reid and that it would be important for him to respond to any outreach from them.<br>On <b>2/10/25</b>, I confirmed with our third-party data partner that Reid’s application was successfully submitted via eFax to the hospital using the fax number listed on their financial assistance application.<br>A couple of weeks later, on <b>2/25</b>, Reid emailed me to let me know that CommonSpirit had tried calling him several times. I attempted to reach him the following day (<b>2/26</b>) and left a voicemail reiterating that if the hospital called, he should connect with them directly.<br>I followed up again on <b>3/12</b> to check in. Reid explained that CommonSpirit was in the middle of transitioning their billing to Advent Health and that they were unable to access billing information at that time. He was advised to wait until mid-March for a new bill.<br>On <b>3/20</b>, Reid received a bill from US Acute Care, which was for physician services and separate from the hospital. I looked into this entity and confirmed that they honor the hospital’s financial aid determinations once approved. Because the hospital bill was still pending and the Acute Care bill was not yet due, I recommended waiting so we could address everything together.<br>On <b>4/5</b>, Reid shared that he had contacted CommonSpirit billing and was told accounts were on hold due to the billing transition, nothing would be sent to collections, and updated bills were expected around May 1. I advised him to let US Acute Care know he was awaiting a hospital financial aid determination.<br>A week later, on <b>4/12</b>, Reid emailed to say he had contacted US Acute Care and made an initial payment, with the plan to pursue dismissal and a refund once the hospital bill was finalized.<br>Reid received what appeared to be an updated CommonSpirit bill on <b>4/17</b>, so we called the hospital’s financial assistance department together on <b>4/22</b>. At that time, they advised the transition was still ongoing and they could not access any patient financial accounts. I asked Reid to let me know once a finalized bill came in so we could try again.<br>In <b>June</b>, Reid believed he had received the final bill and we called CommonSpirit together again on <b>6/20</b>. Unfortunately, we were told once more that, despite bills being mailed, financial accounts were still inaccessible and that we needed to continue waiting for a new bill.<br>Finally, on <b>1/5/26</b>, Reid reached out to say he had received what he believed was the final bill. Given the repeated direction we had received from the hospital and the self-service nature of the program, I asked if he felt comfortable calling the hospital directly, which he agreed to do. During that call, the hospital advised they had no record of receiving the original application submitted back in February 2025.<br>At Reid’s request, I then provided him with all documentation that had been submitted originally and in addition to resubmitting his application on <b>1/7/26</b>.<br>For additional context, Debt Dismissal is designed to be a self-service program. Once an application is submitted, the hospital communicates directly with the patient regarding eligibility and next steps. ScriptSourcing does not determine financial aid approval, our role is to assist with submission and guidance throughout the process.<br>CommonSpirit did attempt to reach Reid shortly after the original submission in February, and as discussed during our initial call, those communications were meant to go directly to him. It’s possible this could have been resolved earlier had those calls connected. Once the hospital entered its billing transition, access issues compounded the delays despite repeated follow-up efforts from both Reid as well as us together.<br>After reviewing this internally, leadership confirmed that since ScriptSourcing does not collect the $50 monthly fee, we’re unable to issue a refund. While the program has been successful overall, timely communication between the patient and the hospital is a critical part of the process.<br>I’ve attached a few screenshots that show key outreach efforts and communication, and I’m happy to forward any relevant email threads as well if that would be helpful.<br>Please let me know if you have any questions or would like to talk through any part of this further.<br><br>Best,<br>Sarah Shellhorn', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2026-02-06 15:26:37', '2026-02-06 15:30:42'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000180284001', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000120893013', 'Ashley Sargent', NULL, '2-6-26 I noticed Ashley doesn''t have wellness benfits and I know she wants them. We added them for March 1st. I just sent this to Patti regarding the requested changes.<br><span><br></span>Hi Patti, Ashley Sargent just submitted two Change Forms. You can disregard the first one. The 2nd one is the accurate one with adding the MEC and Changing the IUA to $1250. She''s self-employed and her most important aspect of the membership is the wellness benefits. So, she wants Secure HSA at $1250 IUA.  Sorry for the confusion. Thank you!<br><span><br></span>Ashley''s new cost will be $359/$1250 for March 1st onward. She used her EIN.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2026-02-06 15:11:06', '2026-02-06 15:11:06'),
